      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Albrecht Karle",
        "Francis Halzen",
        "Shigeru Yoshida"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The detection of high-energy astrophysical neutrinos established neutrino astronomy and opened a new observational window on the universe.",
        "attribution": "Halzen led the observatory and its scientific program; Karle and Yoshida made central contributions to detector development and the analyses that established the signal.",
        "maturity": "The astrophysical flux has been confirmed through sustained observations, with further source identifications strengthening the field.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The source population responsible for most of the diffuse flux remains incompletely identified.",
          "A prize centered on three people would necessarily simplify the work of a large collaboration."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B004",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Maura McLaughlin",
        "Michael Kramer",
        "Richard N. Manchester"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Pulsar timing evidence for a nanohertz gravitational-wave background opens a frequency range inaccessible to ground-based interferometers and extends gravitational-wave astronomy.",
        "attribution": "These researchers helped establish the long-baseline pulsar timing programs and methods underlying the result; credit must account for the international array collaborations.",
        "maturity": "Multiple timing collaborations have reported compatible evidence, making the background result persuasive, though the field is still developing.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The background's astrophysical interpretation and the contributions of individual source populations remain unsettled.",
          "Evidence for the background is firmer than identification of its sources."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B001",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Alan H. Guth",
        "Andrei Linde",
        "Viatcheslav Mukhanov"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Inflation and the quantum origin of primordial structure offer a powerful account of early-universe conditions and connect fundamental theory to observable cosmology.",
        "attribution": "Guth and Linde developed influential inflationary frameworks; Mukhanov derived the quantum fluctuations that provide a basis for structure formation.",
        "maturity": "The framework has shaped decades of observations, but its distinctive mechanism has not been directly established.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "No unique inflationary model or decisive signature has been confirmed.",
          "The broad range of models makes it difficult to treat inflation as one experimentally established discovery."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B010",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Alexander A. Belavin",
        "Alexander B. Zamolodchikov",
        "Alexander M. Polyakov"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Two-dimensional conformal field theory supplied exact tools for understanding critical phenomena and influenced statistical mechanics and quantum field theory profoundly.",
        "attribution": "The credited work established central methods and results; the contributions are tightly connected, although the broader development drew on a wider theoretical community.",
        "maturity": "The framework has had decades of use and remains a durable part of modern theoretical physics.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The reach of the work is clear, while the boundary of the prize-worthy contribution relative to earlier conformal symmetry work is less sharp."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B028",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Hidetoshi Katori",
        "Jun Ye"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical lattice clocks have transformed precision timekeeping and tests of fundamental physics, with a clear path to broad scientific and practical impact.",
        "attribution": "Katori and Ye independently pioneered complementary clock approaches that established the field.",
        "maturity": "The performance gains are repeatable and well established; clock comparisons and applications continue to improve.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The field has many major contributors, so the prize citation would need to define the decisive early advances carefully."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B005",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Alexei Kitaev",
        "Andrew M. Steane",
        "Peter W. Shor"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Quantum error correction showed how quantum information can be protected against noise and made fault-tolerant quantum computation physically conceivable.",
        "attribution": "Shor and Steane developed landmark codes, while Kitaev's topological approach connected protection to a powerful physical framework.",
        "maturity": "The ideas have been tested in increasingly capable experiments, including logical error suppression, but large-scale fault-tolerant computation remains unachieved.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The ultimate technological impact is still unknown, and the boundary between foundational theory and later experimental realization deserves care."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
